1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,363
This is ECE 320, video 18. The topic is virtual memory. It is unit 7 of the slides.

2
00:00:08,813 --> 00:00:19,778
After this video, you can do four things. One: split a virtual address into the virtual page number and the page offset, and translate it with a page table.

3
00:00:20,228 --> 00:00:27,396
Two: calculate the size of a page table. This includes the two-level page table of assignment 6.

4
00:00:27,846 --> 00:00:33,499
Three: explain the translation buffer, translation buffer misses and page faults.

5
00:00:33,949 --> 00:00:42,120
Four: compare virtual, physical and virtual-physical caches, and explain the parallel T L B and cache access.

6
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:57,546
A program uses virtual addresses, or V A. They go from 0 to 2 to the N, minus 1. N is the machine size, for example 32 bits for embedded systems or 64 bits for servers.

7
00:00:57,996 --> 00:01:11,287
Memory uses physical addresses, or P A. They go from 0 to 2 to the M, minus 1. Usually M is smaller than N. 2 to the M is the most memory that the machine supports.

8
00:01:11,737 --> 00:01:24,537
The mapping from V A to P A works on pages. A page is a block of addresses with a fixed size. Virtual page 0, the first code page, maps to physical page 3.

9
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:38,469
The mapping does not have to keep pages in order. Virtual page 1 is next to virtual page 0, but it maps to physical page 0. Virtual page 2 maps to physical page 1.

10
00:01:38,919 --> 00:01:47,879
A virtual page does not have to map to any physical page. Virtual pages 3 and 4 are not used, so they have no mapping.

11
00:01:48,329 --> 00:01:57,673
Virtual pages that are not in memory live on disk, in the swap area. Here the stack page, virtual page 5, is on the disk.

12
00:01:58,123 --> 00:02:12,651
This is the basic idea of virtual memory: memory acts like a cache for the disk. It holds a changing subset of the program's pages. The operating system manages this, and the program does not see it.

13
00:02:13,101 --> 00:02:28,376
Why was virtual memory made? The original reason was compatibility. IBM System 370 was a family of computers with one set of software. Virtual memory is older than caches, by a little.

14
00:02:28,826 --> 00:02:31,706
One machine of the family had a small memory.

15
00:02:32,156 --> 00:02:34,460
Another machine had a large memory.

16
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:45,342
With virtual memory, the same program runs on both machines. On each machine, the program sees 2 to the N bytes, for any amount of real memory.

17
00:02:45,792 --> 00:02:52,043
Before virtual memory, the programmers had to write their code for the memory size of the machine.

18
00:02:52,493 --> 00:03:00,471
The word virtual means: in effect, but not in actuality. The memory appears to be 2 to the N bytes, but it is not.

19
00:03:00,921 --> 00:03:12,911
Virtual memory has other uses. The first is multiprogramming: many processes run on one machine. Each process thinks that it has 2 to the N bytes of address space.

20
00:03:13,361 --> 00:03:27,846
The system maps the virtual pages of different processes to different physical pages. Program 0 gets physical pages 0 to 2. Program 1 gets 3 to 5. Program 2 gets 6 and 7.

21
00:03:28,296 --> 00:03:36,701
Each process can reach only its own physical pages. So a process cannot read or write the memory of another process.

22
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:50,783
The second use is inter-process communication. The system maps virtual pages of two processes to the same physical page. Here Program 0 and Program 2 share physical page 8.

23
00:03:51,233 --> 00:04:03,308
The third use is protection at the level of pages. The operating system gives each page a set of permissions: read, write and execute. The hardware enforces them.

24
00:04:03,758 --> 00:04:10,606
Virtual page 0 holds code. It has read and execute permission, but no write permission.

25
00:04:11,056 --> 00:04:17,669
Virtual page 1 holds data. It has read and write permission, but no execute permission.

26
00:04:18,119 --> 00:04:27,933
Now the program tries to store a word to virtual page 0. The hardware checks the write permission of virtual page 0. It is not set.

27
00:04:28,383 --> 00:04:34,377
So the hardware raises an exception. The operating system then terminates the program.

28
00:04:34,827 --> 00:04:40,779
The same happens when the program tries to execute data, or to write a read-only page.

29
00:04:41,229 --> 00:04:55,160
The mapping from V A to P A is called address translation. Slide 7 gives an example: a 32-bit machine, so the virtual address has 32 bits, bit 31 to bit 0.

30
00:04:55,610 --> 00:05:09,178
The pages are 64 kilobytes. That is 2 to the 16 bytes. So the low 16 bits select a byte in the page. They are the page offset, P O F S, bits 15 to 0.

31
00:05:09,628 --> 00:05:18,695
The other bits are the virtual page number, V P N. 32 minus 16 is 16 bits, bits 31 to 16.

32
00:05:19,145 --> 00:05:28,574
The maximum memory is 256 megabytes. That is 2 to the 28 bytes. So the physical address has 28 bits.

33
00:05:29,024 --> 00:05:36,405
The page offset is not translated. The hardware copies it into bits 15 to 0 of the physical address.

34
00:05:36,855 --> 00:05:48,717
Only the V P N is translated, into the physical page number, P P N. It has 28 minus 16, which is 12 bits, bits 27 to 16.

35
00:05:49,167 --> 00:05:55,161
So there are 2 to the 16 virtual pages, but only 2 to the 12 physical pages.

36
00:05:55,611 --> 00:06:06,982
How are addresses translated? Each process gets a page table, P T. It is stored in physical memory. Each row is a page table entry, P T E.

37
00:06:07,432 --> 00:06:15,325
A page table entry points to a location. It is a physical page in memory, or a place on the swap disk.

38
00:06:15,775 --> 00:06:22,175
The page table is indexed by the V P N. So the row number is the virtual page number.

39
00:06:22,625 --> 00:06:39,649
Here is our example, with the split of slide 7. The virtual address is 0 x 0 0 0 3 1 2 3 4. The top 16 bits give V P N 3. The low 16 bits give the page offset 1 2 3 4.

40
00:06:40,099 --> 00:06:48,099
Use V P N 3 as the row number. Row 3 of the page table holds the entry for virtual page 3.

41
00:06:48,549 --> 00:06:56,144
The entry gives P P N 0 x 0 5 A. It goes into the top 12 bits of the physical address.

42
00:06:56,594 --> 00:07:02,503
The page offset 1 2 3 4 is copied unchanged into the low 16 bits.

43
00:07:02,953 --> 00:07:10,719
So the physical address is 0 x 0 5 A 1 2 3 4. One process has one page table.

44
00:07:11,169 --> 00:07:24,353
Slide 9 asks: how big is a page table on this machine? It has 32-bit addresses, 4 kilobyte pages, and 4 byte page table entries. Pause the video and try it.

45
00:07:24,803 --> 00:07:31,480
A page is 4 kilobytes, which is 2 to the 12 bytes. So the page offset has 12 bits.

46
00:07:31,930 --> 00:07:46,437
The V P N has 32 minus 12, which is 20 bits. So there are 2 to the 20 virtual pages, about 1 million. The slide says the same: 4 gigabytes divided by 4 kilobytes.

47
00:07:46,887 --> 00:07:52,967
The page table has one entry for each virtual page. So it has 2 to the 20 rows.

48
00:07:53,417 --> 00:08:01,950
Each entry is 4 bytes. 2 to the 20 entries times 4 bytes is 2 to the 22 bytes. That is 4 megabytes.

49
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:13,195
Each process has its own page table. 100 processes need 100 tables, so 100 times 4 megabytes is 400 megabytes.

50
00:08:13,645 --> 00:08:25,975
So page tables can get very large. Multi-level page tables make them smaller. The O S course covers them, and assignment 6 at the end of this video uses two levels.

51
00:08:26,425 --> 00:08:42,319
Slide 11 asks six questions. What, why and how have answers: translate addresses, for compatibility, multiprogramming and protection, with page tables. Three are open: who translates, when, and where is the page table?

52
00:08:42,769 --> 00:08:53,435
Option 1: the process, that is the program, translates its own addresses. Then the page table is in the virtual address space that the process can see.

53
00:08:53,885 --> 00:09:03,891
Slide 11 gives two problems. First, the program, and the programmer, must know physical addresses. Virtual memory exists to avoid this.

54
00:09:04,341 --> 00:09:10,271
Second, the program can forge physical addresses and change the memory of other programs.

55
00:09:10,721 --> 00:09:20,279
Option 2: the operating system translates for the process. The page table is in the virtual address space of the operating system.

56
00:09:20,729 --> 00:09:24,974
So a user process cannot view or change its own page table.

57
00:09:25,424 --> 00:09:29,371
And a user process does not need to know physical addresses.

58
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:34,023
With option 2, the translation happens on L2 misses.

59
00:09:34,473 --> 00:09:45,033
Here is option 2 step by step. The C P U accesses memory with a virtual address. The L1 and L2 caches use the virtual address.

60
00:09:45,483 --> 00:09:51,094
On an L2 miss, an interrupt transfers control to the operating system handler.

61
00:09:51,544 --> 00:09:57,197
The handler translates the virtual address. It reads the page table of the process.

62
00:09:57,647 --> 00:10:00,869
Then it accesses memory with the physical address.

63
00:10:01,319 --> 00:10:06,055
When the L2 fill completes, the handler returns to the user process.

64
00:10:06,505 --> 00:10:14,313
This is still slow. An interrupt handler runs, and a page table lookup in memory comes before the memory access.

65
00:10:14,763 --> 00:10:20,843
Address translation is still too slow. Remember that the page table is in physical memory.

66
00:10:21,293 --> 00:10:27,607
So the first memory transaction reads the page table entry. This translates the address.

67
00:10:28,057 --> 00:10:33,881
The second memory transaction accesses the page, if the page is in physical memory.

68
00:10:34,331 --> 00:10:37,681
Two memory transactions for each access is slow.

69
00:10:38,131 --> 00:10:50,141
The fix is to cache the translations, in a translation buffer, T B. On a T B hit, the translation is ready at once, and only the page access goes to memory.

70
00:10:50,591 --> 00:10:53,941
The page table is walked only on a T B miss.

71
00:10:54,391 --> 00:11:09,153
In the hierarchy of slide 14, the T B sits after the L2 cache, before main memory. The caches use virtual addresses. The T B turns the virtual address into a physical address for memory.

72
00:11:09,603 --> 00:11:14,403
The T B is a small cache. It has 16 to 64 entries.

73
00:11:14,853 --> 00:11:22,704
Each entry has a tag and data. The tag is a V P N. The data is the P P N for that V P N.

74
00:11:23,154 --> 00:11:33,117
The T B is usually fully associative. A lookup compares the V P N with every tag. Here we look up V P N 3 from our example.

75
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:40,713
The second entry has tag 3. That is a hit, and the T B gives P P N 0 x 0 5 A.

76
00:11:41,163 --> 00:11:54,049
The T B uses temporal locality in the page table accesses: a page that was used is used again soon. And the operating system handler runs only on a T B miss.

77
00:11:54,499 --> 00:12:04,077
Each access first looks up the virtual address in the T B. On a hit, the P P N gives the physical address, and the access goes on.

78
00:12:04,527 --> 00:12:13,210
A T B miss means that the translation is not in the T B. It may still be in the page table. So the page table is walked.

79
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:22,279
If the page is in memory, the page table entry is loaded from memory into the T B. Then the instruction is restarted.

80
00:12:22,729 --> 00:12:28,873
If the page is not in memory, this is a page fault. The next slide covers page faults.

81
00:12:29,323 --> 00:12:45,557
There are two methods to walk the page table on a T B miss. Option 1: an operating system routine, as in Alpha, MIPS and ARM. A short routine of about 10 instructions walks the page table and updates the T B.

82
00:12:46,007 --> 00:12:50,103
The page table format is flexible, because software walks it.

83
00:12:50,553 --> 00:12:55,353
But the routine needs an operating system call, and this adds latency.

84
00:12:55,803 --> 00:13:06,257
Option 2: a hardware finite state machine, as in x86. A page table register gives the page table, and the hardware walks it.

85
00:13:06,707 --> 00:13:09,843
This saves the cost of the operating system call.

86
00:13:10,293 --> 00:13:14,154
But the page table format is hard-coded in the hardware.

87
00:13:14,604 --> 00:13:25,612
A page fault is a T B miss where the page is not in physical memory. It starts as a T B miss, and the operating system or hardware handler finds it.

88
00:13:26,062 --> 00:13:36,089
Then an operating system routine runs. First, it chooses a physical page to replace. It uses a software version of L R U.

89
00:13:36,539 --> 00:13:44,176
If the chosen page is dirty, the routine writes it to disk. This is like a dirty block in a write-back cache.

90
00:13:44,626 --> 00:13:57,661
Then the operating system reads the missing page from disk and puts it in physical memory. This takes about 10 milliseconds. So the operating system runs another task in the meantime.

91
00:13:58,111 --> 00:14:05,492
When the read completes, the page table is updated. Then the access is handled like a normal T B miss.

92
00:14:05,942 --> 00:14:21,473
Now, where does the translation happen? Slide 17 shows the hierarchy so far: virtual caches. The caches are indexed and tagged with virtual addresses. The T B translates only to access physical memory.

93
00:14:21,923 --> 00:14:27,107
This is fast. In the common case, a cache hit, no translation is needed.

94
00:14:27,557 --> 00:14:42,149
But on a context switch, the next process uses the same virtual addresses for other data. So the caches must be flushed, which is slow. Or a process I D, P I D, is added to the cache tags.

95
00:14:42,599 --> 00:14:57,404
And inter-process communication is difficult. With aliasing, two virtual addresses map to the same physical address. Then two copies can be in the cache, so shared memory cannot be cached. This is very slow.

96
00:14:57,854 --> 00:15:09,246
Slide 18 shows the other choice: physical caches. The T B comes first and translates at once. The caches are indexed and tagged with physical addresses.

97
00:15:09,696 --> 00:15:16,907
On a context switch, the caches need no flush. Different processes do not share physical addresses.

98
00:15:17,357 --> 00:15:25,783
Inter-process communication can use the cache. A shared page has one physical address, so there is one copy in the cache.

99
00:15:26,233 --> 00:15:33,508
But the hit time increases. The T B is on the critical path of every access, also in the common case.

100
00:15:33,958 --> 00:15:43,835
Slide 19 shows the compromise: virtual-physical caches. The cache is indexed with the virtual address, and tagged with the physical address.

101
00:15:44,285 --> 00:15:48,616
So the cache access and the address translation happen in parallel.

102
00:15:49,066 --> 00:15:58,282
There are no context switch or aliasing problems, because the tags are physical. And it is fast: there are no extra hit time cycles.

103
00:15:58,732 --> 00:16:09,740
A T B that works in parallel with a cache is called a translation lookaside buffer, T L B. This organization is common in processors today.

104
00:16:10,190 --> 00:16:28,857
Slide 20 shows how the T L B and the cache work in parallel. It uses 32-bit addresses. There are two ways to look at the virtual address. The T L B sees the V P N, bits 31 to 16, and the page offset, bits 15 to 0.

105
00:16:29,307 --> 00:16:39,248
The cache sees a tag, bits 31 to 12, an index, bits 11 to 5, and a block offset, bits 4 to 0. The tag is physical.

106
00:16:39,698 --> 00:16:43,751
The index and the block offset lie inside the page offset.

107
00:16:44,201 --> 00:16:52,500
Address translation does not change the page offset. So the index bits are the same in the virtual and the physical address.

108
00:16:52,950 --> 00:17:03,062
This gives the rule: the V P N and the index must not overlap. Here the V P N ends at bit 16, and the index starts at bit 11.

109
00:17:03,512 --> 00:17:12,771
So the virtual address goes to both units at the same time. The V P N goes to the T L B. The index selects the cache set.

110
00:17:13,221 --> 00:17:25,957
The T L B gives the P P N, the physical tag. It is compared with the tags of the set. This gives the cache hit or miss. The T L B also gives its own hit or miss.

111
00:17:26,407 --> 00:17:38,780
Like caches, T L Bs have associativity, block size and capacity. The associativity is at least 4-way. Fully associative T L Bs are common.

112
00:17:39,230 --> 00:17:46,206
What does a block size of two mean for a T L B? Two consecutive virtual pages share one tag.

113
00:17:46,656 --> 00:17:54,912
Here is our example. Take V P N 6 and V P N 7. In binary they are 1 1 0 and 1 1 1.

114
00:17:55,362 --> 00:18:02,167
They differ only in the lowest bit. The bits above it are 1 1 for both. So both have tag 3.

115
00:18:02,617 --> 00:18:06,223
The lowest bit selects one of the two pages in the block.

116
00:18:06,673 --> 00:18:16,379
So one T L B entry holds tag 3 and two P P Ns: one for virtual page 6 and one for virtual page 7.

117
00:18:16,829 --> 00:18:20,819
Also like caches, there can be L2 T L Bs.

118
00:18:21,269 --> 00:18:36,287
Virtual memory is almost everywhere today, at least in general-purpose processors. It has several forms. Paging, also called flat memory, uses translation blocks of equal size. Most systems do this.

119
00:18:36,737 --> 00:18:46,615
Segmentation uses translation blocks of variable size. IA 32 uses it. The slide says that it makes life very difficult.

120
00:18:47,065 --> 00:18:52,163
A third form, paged segments, is named on the slide but not explained.

121
00:18:52,613 --> 00:19:11,280
Now assignment 6, question 1. A virtual memory system has a 32-bit virtual address, 4 kilobyte pages, and 4 byte page table entries. Part a: what is the total page table size for 5 applications? Pause the video and try it.

122
00:19:11,730 --> 00:19:21,202
A page has 4096 bytes. Log 2 of 4096 is 12. So the page offset has 12 bits.

123
00:19:21,652 --> 00:19:32,895
There are 2 to the 32 addresses. Divide by 2 to the 12 bytes per page: 2 to the 20 pages. So each page table has 2 to the 20 entries.

124
00:19:33,345 --> 00:19:41,793
One table is 2 to the 20 entries times 4 bytes, which is 4 megabytes. Each application has its own table.

125
00:19:42,243 --> 00:19:49,752
So the total is 5 applications times 2 to the 20 entries times 4 bytes. That is 20 megabytes.

126
00:19:50,202 --> 00:20:17,274
Part b uses a two-level page table. The page size stays the same. There are 256 second-level tables, and a first-level table points to them. A first-level entry is 6 bytes. A second-level entry is 4 bytes. Find the total size for 5 applications when 50 percent, and when 100 percent, of the second-level tables are used. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:20:17,724 --> 00:20:24,828
The offset stays 12 bits, and the page number stays 20 bits. Now the 20 bits are split in two.

128
00:20:25,278 --> 00:20:35,049
The first-level bits select one of 256 second-level tables. Log 2 of 256 is 8. So 8 bits.

129
00:20:35,499 --> 00:20:49,600
The other 20 minus 8, which is 12 bits, select the entry in the second-level table. So each second-level table has 2 to the 20, divided by 2 to the 8, which is 2 to the 12 entries.

130
00:20:50,050 --> 00:21:03,085
The first-level table has 256 entries of 6 bytes. 256 times 6 is 1536 bytes, which is 1.5 kilobytes.

131
00:21:03,535 --> 00:21:10,191
One second-level table has 2 to the 12 entries of 4 bytes. That is 16 kilobytes.

132
00:21:10,641 --> 00:21:16,507
A second-level table that is not used is left out. Its first-level entry is NULL.

133
00:21:16,957 --> 00:21:29,267
Case 1: 50 percent of the second-level tables are used. That is 128 of 256. So 128 first-level entries are not NULL.

134
00:21:29,717 --> 00:21:43,114
Each used second-level table is 2 to the 12 entries times 4 bytes, which is 16 kilobytes. 128 times 16 kilobytes is 2 megabytes for each application.

135
00:21:43,564 --> 00:21:51,777
The first-level table is always complete: 256 entries times 6 bytes is 1.5 kilobytes.

136
00:21:52,227 --> 00:22:05,838
Each application needs 2 megabytes plus 1.5 kilobytes. For 5 applications: 10 megabytes plus 7.5 kilobytes. That is about 10 point 0 1 megabytes.

137
00:22:06,288 --> 00:22:14,629
Case 2: 100 percent are used. All 256 first-level entries point to a second-level table.

138
00:22:15,079 --> 00:22:21,159
256 times 16 kilobytes is 4 megabytes for each application.

139
00:22:21,609 --> 00:22:26,452
The first-level table is the same as before: 1.5 kilobytes.

140
00:22:26,902 --> 00:22:38,742
5 times, 4 megabytes plus 1.5 kilobytes, is 20 megabytes plus 7.5 kilobytes. That is about 20 point 0 1 megabytes.

141
00:22:39,192 --> 00:22:53,635
Compare with part a. When only half of the tables are used, two levels need about half the memory. When all are used, two levels need 1.5 kilobytes more per application, for the first-level table.

142
00:22:54,085 --> 00:23:12,303
Here is the recap. One. Programs use virtual addresses, from 0 to 2 to the N, minus 1. Memory uses physical addresses, from 0 to 2 to the M, minus 1. Virtual memory gives compatibility, multiprogramming, sharing and protection.

143
00:23:12,753 --> 00:23:25,532
Two. A virtual address is a V P N and a page offset. Only the V P N is translated, to the P P N. The number of offset bits is log 2 of the page size.

144
00:23:25,982 --> 00:23:37,907
Three. Each process has a page table, indexed by the V P N. Its size is 2 to the number of V P N bits, times the size of a page table entry.

145
00:23:38,357 --> 00:23:50,069
Four. With two levels, a complete first-level table points to the second-level tables. Unused second-level tables are left out, and their first-level entries are NULL.

146
00:23:50,519 --> 00:24:13,581
Five. The T B is a small, fully associative cache of translations. Its tag is the V P N and its data is the P P N. On a T B miss, an operating system routine or a hardware state machine walks the page table. If the page is not in memory, it is a page fault, which takes about 10 milliseconds.

147
00:24:14,031 --> 00:24:29,071
Six. Virtual-physical caches use the virtual address for the index and the physical address for the tag, with the T L B in parallel. The index must lie inside the page offset. That is the end of video 18.
